    <h2>James 2: "Faith Without Works Is Dead" Explained</h2>
    <p>James is addressing believers about profitable faith toward others — not how to obtain eternal salvation.</p>
    <div class="verse">James 2:14-26 context is practical: helping a brother in need. Faith without works doesn't profit others.</div>
    <p><strong>Rahab the Harlot:</strong> Justified by faith (Hebrews 11:31, James 2:25). No record of her repenting of her whoredom as a requirement for salvation. She believed God and hid the spies.</p>
